    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes with `BUILD SUCCESS` and all tests green. Here's a summary of the soft-delete implementation.\n\n## Changes\n\n**Model & persistence**\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added a `deleted` field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`, `Boolean`, defaulting to `false`) with getter/setter, so a newly created owner is `false`.\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` column to the `owners` table.\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 the seed rows use positional `INSERT ... VALUES`, so I appended `FALSE` to each of the 10 owner rows (h2/postgres already use named-column inserts and needed no change).\n\n**Endpoint behavior**\n- `rest/function/owner/DeleteOwner.java` \u2014 `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` now sets `deleted = true` and `save()`s the owner instead of removing it. The pipeline still responds `204` (2xx) and the row is retained, so `GET` returns it with `deleted: true`.\n\n**Create-endpoint checks ignore deleted owners**\n- `RequireUniqueIdentity.java` \u2014 skips any owner flagged `deleted` when checking contact/household duplicates, so a duplicate is allowed when the only match was deleted.\n- `FlagPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 skips deleted owners when computing the possible-duplicate flag.\n\n**API contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema (regenerates the `OwnerDto`, which MapStruct maps automatically so `GET` returns the field).\n\n**Consistency for the JDBC profile** (not used by the test profile, which is `spring-data-jpa`, but kept correct): `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` now selects and persists `deleted`, and its `delete()` performs a soft-delete `UPDATE`.\n\nTest results: `AcceptanceTest` (2 tests \u2014 `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck`) pass, full suite `BUILD SUCCESS`, exit 0.",
